         <div type="bio">
            <p>British publisher. Mathews and partner John Lane founded the Bodley Head press in 1887. His disagreement with Lane over the press’s publication of the risqué <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Yellow Book</name> magazine led him to form his own firm in 1894. Mathews became one of London’s leading poetry publishers, issuing titles by <name type="person" key="yeat65">Yeats</name>, <name type="person" key="aldi92">Aldington</name>, and Masefield; he was also a supporter of the Irish Literary Revival, publishing <name type="person" key="syng71">Synge</name>’s <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Riders to the Sea</name>. <name type="person" key="symo65">Arthur Symons</name> brought JJ’s collection of verse, <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Chamber Music</name>, to Mathews’s attention; he published the book in 1907. Within months of the book’s appearance, JJ tried to interest Mathews in <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Dubliners</name>, but was refused. In 1913 he again offered <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Dubliners</name> to Mathews, and was again refused, although Mathews did eventually reissue <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Chamber Music</name> in 1918. Mathews was <name type="person" key="poun85">Ezra Pound</name>’s most important early publisher; until Mathews’s death in 1921 and the sale of his firm, he published some half dozen of the poet’s books. <hi rend="bold">William Brockman</hi>
                </p>
         </div>
